From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dxva2: DXVA-HD: a video processor on the Direct3D 9Ex device.

DXVAHD_CreateDevice was a stub (E_NOTIMPL), so a player doing its video processing through DXVA-HD (the
Direct3D 9 video processing API of Windows 7 on) had none. Now it gives an IDXVAHD_Device on the
application's Direct3D 9Ex device: capabilities (one video processor, one input stream, no filters or
deinterlacing), output (X8R8G8B8, A8R8G8B8) and input formats (NV12, YUY2, YV12 and the RGB ones), video
surfaces (render targets for output, offscreen plain surfaces for input), and an IDXVAHD_VideoProcessor
whose blit and stream states are kept and checked by size, as documented. VideoProcessBltHD does what
dxva2's progressive video processor does: ColorFill of the target rectangle with the background colour
(YCbCr converted), then StretchRect of the stream's source rectangle into its destination rectangle,
letting the device convert YUV to RGB.
---
--- a/dlls/dxva2/Makefile.in
+++ b/dlls/dxva2/Makefile.in
@@ -5,4 +5,5 @@
 EXTRADLLFLAGS = -Wb,--prefer-native
 
 SOURCES = \
+	dxvahd.c \
 	main.c
--- a/dlls/dxva2/main.c
+++ b/dlls/dxva2/main.c
@@ -1614,10 +1614,4 @@
     return monitor_no_ddcci(monitor);
 }
 
-HRESULT WINAPI DXVAHD_CreateDevice(IDirect3DDevice9Ex *d3d9_device, const DXVAHD_CONTENT_DESC *desc,
-        DXVAHD_DEVICE_USAGE usage, PDXVAHDSW_Plugin plugin, IDXVAHD_Device **device)
-{
-    FIXME("d3d9_device %p, desc %p, usage %u, plugin %p, device %p, stub!\n", d3d9_device, desc, usage, plugin, device);
-
-    return E_NOTIMPL;
-}
+/* DXVAHD_CreateDevice: dxvahd.c (Stained Glass OS, wine-sg 8207) */
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dlls/dxva2/dxvahd.c
@@ -0,0 +1,552 @@
